        {
            "block_label": "abstract",
            "block_content": "Despite recent declines in cardiovascular mortality,\natherosclerotic disease is still the major health\nproblem facing Western society. With the identification\nof predisposing risk factors, the responsibility\nfor avoiding the atherosclerotic diseases has become\na personal endeavor requiring an alteration in life-\nstyle. The most firmly established risk factors for\ncoronary heart disease (CHD) are hypercholesterola-\nemia, hypertension, and cigarette smoking (Fig. 1).\nIn addition to these, diabetes, lack of exercise,\nobesity, and type A behavior all contribute to the\nmultifactorial atherogenic process. Not all physi-\nciants are convinced, however, that CHD is prevent-\nable by correcting these risk factors, and some claim\nthat the cigarette-CHD connection especially is\nactually a spurious illustration of guilt by association.\nA review of the evidence incriminating cigarette smoking in the development of atherosclerosis\nin general and CHD in particular would seem in-\norder.",
            "block_bbox": [
                4,
                279,
                292,
                588
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    4.0,
                    279.0
                ],
                [
                    292.0,
                    279.0
                ],
                [
                    292.0,
                    588.0
                ],
                [
                    4.0,
                    588.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "MAGNITUDE OF THE PROBLEM",
            "block_bbox": [
                4,
                596,
                175,
                612
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    4.0,
                    596.0
                ],
                [
                    174.0,
                    596.0
                ],
                [
                    174.0,
                    611.0
                ],
                [
                    4.0,
                    611.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Despite the fact that as early as 1938 cigarette smokers were shown to die at an earlier age, and a massive accumulation of subsequent data detailing the multiple hazards of cigarette smoking, almost 60 million Americans continue to smoke cigarettes and teenagers continue to take up the habit. It is estimated that the cigarette habit directly contributes to $325,000 premature deaths each year. The health care costs are estimated to be $8.2 billion, and host earnings are estimated at $19 billion. Although the per capita consumption of cigarettes appears to be declining, about 40% of adults still smoke. Although the relative risk of cigarette smoking is greater for lung cancer, the absolute risk is greater for coronary disease and smoking-related coronary deaths amount to some 250,000 yearly compared to 70,000 for cancer. The attributable risks for cigarette smoking are substantial, due to its high prevalence in the general population as well as its potency as a cardiovascular risk factor (Table 1).",
            "block_bbox": [
                1,
                618,
                289,
                805
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    1.0,
                    618.0
                ],
