        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "THE CARDIOVASCULAR HAZARDS",
            "block_bbox": [
                308,
                414,
                496,
                430
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    308.0,
                    414.0
                ],
                [
                    495.0,
                    414.0
                ],
                [
                    495.0,
                    429.0
                ],
                [
                    308.0,
                    429.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Cigarette smokers have twice the overall death rate of non-smokers in most of Western societies. The Framingham Study data are fairly representative of prospective epidemiologic evidence on the association between cigarette smoking and cardiovascular morbidity and mortality. Heavy smoking almost doubles cardiovascular and overall mortality under age 65 (Table II). Beyond age 65, neither significant nor substantial gradients of risk can be demonstrated in either sex for cardiovascular morbidity. In men, significant associations of cardiovascular mortality and overall mortality are noted in relation to cigarette smoking, even beyond age 65 (Table III). In women, no significant relationship can be shown. This may be related to the fact that many of these elderly women who smoke do not inhale.",
            "block_bbox": [
                303,
                436,
                595,
                698
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    303.0,
                    436.0
                ],
                [
                    595.0,
                    436.0
                ],
                [
                    595.0,
                    698.0
                ],
                [
                    303.0,
                    698.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "i. Sudden death, in particular, is strongly related to cigarette smoking (Fig. 2). Coronary mortality in general and sudden death specifically are related to cigarette smoking. The proportion of coronary heart disease deaths that are sudden increases progressively with the number of cigarettes smoked per day. Thus fatal coronary attacks occur more commonly in cigarette smokers and when they occur they are more likely to terminate suddenly.",
            "block_bbox": [
                303,
                697,
                593,
                836
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 10,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    303.0,
                    697.0
                ],
                [
                    593.0,
                    697.0
                ],
                [
                    593.0,
                    836.0
                ],
                [
                    303.0,
                    836.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Cardiovascular morbidity. Not only are cardiovascular events likely to terminate fatally more often in cigarette smokers, but such attacks occurred more commonly than in non-smokers. Atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease incidence in general and each of its major clinical manifestations are related to",
